ing is learning to  control one agent but there may be many  settings in which you actually want to  well a number of agents together and one  example of this is modeling traffic so  if you think about every there could  there's a Paula there could be a policy  controlling every single traffic light  deciding when it should be green or when  it should be red every car might be its  own agent as well and in this setting  the interesting thing is not necessarily  just controlling one of these agents  individually but how you can control  them all together in order to optimize  traffic so here's a quick overview of a  number of the different algorithm  reinforcement learning algorithms that  we've implemented in RL lib and you'll  notice that a bunch of these are  actually community contributions all of  this is open source and and so  contributing a new algorithm is as  simple as opening a pull request and  because these are all building on top of  building blocks for distributed  reinforcement learning this is doesn't  mean reimplemented the full algorithm  from scratch there's a lot of code reuse  here this is integrated with Ray tune so  hyper parameter search is super  important for for reinforcement learning  but they can be difficult to compose  together when you want to do distributed  reinforcement learning inside of a  distributed hyper parameter search and  you know if your reinforcement learning  and hyper parameter search are two  separate distributed systems it can be  hard to nest them inside of each other  or compose them in that way but when  they're built on top of a single  general-purpose system then it's easier  to do that the last thing I want to say  about reinforcement learning for RL lib  is that it is ships with part of sage  makers so if you use Amazon sage maker  you can use RL lib as part of that one  last library I want to talk about is  modem which is a library for speeding up  pandas if you use pandas for data  science this involves changing a single  line of code so pant so data processing  this is a live room where building again  on top of the core array abstractions of  tasks and actors again systems like  spark or Hadoop for distributed data  processing  typically built as standalone  distributed systems so the fact that  we're able to build this on top of Ray  is again testament to the generality of  Ray they're typically what's the problem  here there are different tools for doing  data science and data processing some of  them like pandas work well with small  data on your laptop but pandas isn't  going to run on a cluster and similarly  their tools like spark which are great  for huge data but aren't going to speed  things up on I'll sing on your laptop  beyond pandas and some Odin bridges that  gap if you have a line like import  pandas as PD somewhere in your code base  you can replace that with import mode in  pandas as PD and that's the only change  you would need to make so I will and  here's an example of just a quick  benchmark for reading a large CSV file  as a function of the size of the CSV  file on a single large machine so as you  can imagine the larger the file the  longer pandas takes and if you're using  mode and instead it's able to leverage  multiple cores to read those files much  more quickly and this is a difference  between you know 10 15 seconds and a 7  or so minutes so I'll conclude by saying  that again ray is open source it's  intended to be a general-purpose  distributed system and we've seen that  we can take things that are  traditionally standalone distributed  systems like reinforcement learning  hyper parameter tuning distributed data  processing and implement all of those as  libraries on top of ray and what that  allows is that applications that need to  combine these different things can do so  on top of ray we can implement and  support these cross-cutting applications  that touch many different computational  patterns so it's all on github I'd  encourage you to check it out  oh thanks a lot  really good talk thanks for sharing it  so would you advocate raise use even in  single terminal settings just because  you can use Python across multiple  course so yes if you have more than one  or two if you have a certainly for large  machines if you have more than four  cores than absolutely so a typical way  that we'll do development is we'll start  we'll start just prototyping things in  Python single threaded then I'll  parallel parallel why's that across  multiple cores on my laptop or on a  single machine using Ray and that's  typically just adding a few annotations  and then when I rot when that's too big  for my laptop or for a single machine  then I'll take that and run the same  code on a on a cluster and so there's  sort of a seamless transition from like  single threaded to parallel on a single  machine to the cluster setting and  instead of having to sort of rewrite the  code at that stage and the single  machine case is especially important  because there are many large machines  you can start up a big you know instance  on ec2 that has like a hundred cores or  something like that so yeah that's a  very important use case all right any  other question thanks for the talk  question about out just future support  for the platform do you guys plan on you  know keep developing further and  supporting in the future and yes what  that looks like  yes so yeah the plan is to keep working  on it and to make it really good and to  grow the community around it to grow to  grow the community of developers and  users and and these things beeston  a lot of the development is at Berkeley  but there are actually a number of  contributions from different companies  using Ray as well and the and again the  plan is to yeah to have more development  from that's an important metric we look  at just external contributors  is the plan to take this to like an  Apache or a Linux Foundation or is it  we're open to it if there's a comp